# Q10 — With HTTPS_PROXY set and no direct route out, do provider calls and remote MCP servers work?
Date: 2026-10-13. Network q10-int (`docker network create --internal`).
Proxy q10-proxy: throwaway CONNECT logger on q10-int + bridge (untracked
/tmp script, ~90 lines; logs every REQ line to stdout/file).
Spike container agency-spike-q10 (agency-base:2.0.26) on q10-int ONLY with
HTTPS_PROXY=http://q10-proxy:3128, HTTP_PROXY=http://q10-proxy:3128 and NO
NO_PROXY. Config: q10config (bunprobe -> same-net q10-mcp:8377 node MCP,
inetprobe -> https://mcp.context7.com/mcp), SPIKE_MCP_ENABLE=1, SPIKE_INET_ENABLE=1.

Code search first (review/opencode 2.0.26): only
packages/core/src/effect/websocket-constructor.ts honors *_PROXY/NO_PROXY;
no NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY-style switch anywhere in core/ai source. The behavior
below shows the runtime (Bun fetch) honors the proxy env for provider and
MCP HTTPS traffic anyway — the old "no proxy support found" note is
superseded by this run.

## Commands + trimmed output

$ docker exec agency-spike-q10 curl --noproxy '*' --max-time 25 https://opencode.ai/
curl: (6) Could not resolve host: opencode.ai      (egress:000, real 0.1s)
Negative case: direct egress without the proxy fails — curl --noproxy '*'
https://opencode.ai/ returns Could not resolve host (rc=6, egress:000), and
the proxy log shows no CONNECT for that attempt.
$ # (control: the same curl WITH proxy env honored by curl returns 200 via
$ #  CONNECT opencode.ai:443 — the proxy itself has upstream.)

$ curl -X POST /api/session {"title":"q10","agent":"spike",
  "model":{"providerID":"opencode","id":"cat-jam-free"},"location":{...}}
{"data":{"id":"ses_ec8f31794ffenlwNlbScRw2vrM",...}}          # HTTP 200
$ curl -X POST /api/session/<id>/prompt {"text":"Reply with exactly: q10-marker"}
HTTP 200
$ curl -X POST /api/experimental/session/<id>/wait
HTTP 204
$ curl /api/session/<id>/message  (assistant text, trimmed)
TEXT: q10-marker   (finish: stop, error: null)
idle: succeeded
$ # proxy log during the turn:
PROXY REQ: CONNECT opencode.ai:443 HTTP/1.1
# (boot also logged CONNECT models.opencode.ai:443 — catalog/config fetch.)
# -> provider call SUCCEEDS with no direct route, via the proxy.

$ curl -X POST /api/experimental/mcp/inetprobe/connect   # retry after lazy 404
HTTP 204
$ curl /api/mcp
{"data":[{"name":"bunprobe",...},{"name":"inetprobe","status":{"status":"connected"}},...]}
# server log: "mcp connected" server=inetprobe tools=2
$ # proxy log:
PROXY REQ: CONNECT mcp.context7.com:443 HTTP/1.1   (x2)
# -> remote (internet) MCP server connects and lists 2 tools via the proxy.

$ curl /api/mcp  (bunprobe leg)
{"name":"bunprobe","status":{"status":"failed",
  "error":"Error POSTing to endpoint:  (HTTP 501)"}}
# then, after the throwaway proxy gained plain-HTTP forwarding:
# "socket connection was closed unexpectedly (ECONNRESET)".
# Both failures are limits of the throwaway proxy (it 501'd absolute-URI
# HTTP, then broke SSE streaming), not of the client: the log shows the
# client DID send same-net HTTP (POST http://q10-mcp:8377/mcp) to the proxy.
# Same-net MCP reachability itself was proven on the bridge network (q14)
# and on q10-int's sibling runs; the tunneled-HTTPS case above is the
# question's remote leg.

No-NO_PROXY note: with NO_PROXY unset, ALL client HTTP goes to the proxy —
the log also shows local provider probes (GET 127.0.0.1:1234/11434/8000)
and same-net MCP POSTs arriving at the proxy. Stage 1b's egress design must
set NO_PROXY for fleet-internal hosts (dispatcher, same-net MCP).

## Observation
Yes. On an internal network with no direct route (DNS fails, rc=6), both
the provider call (model turn succeeds, CONNECT opencode.ai:443 in the
proxy log) and a remote internet MCP server (connected, tools=2, CONNECT
mcp.context7.com:443) work through HTTPS_PROXY. No NODE_USE_ENV_PROXY-style
switch was needed and none exists in source; the routed-exception fallback
is not needed.

## Verdict: yes (no fallback needed)
